# sudo rule for the Xymon "powerline" server collector.
#
# The Debian/Ubuntu server package installs this file as
#   /etc/sudoers.d/my-xymon-extensions-server    root:root, mode 0440
# with the rule at the bottom COMMENTED OUT, because powerline itself
# ships disabled (POWERLINE_ENABLED=0). A package should not hand out a
# root privilege for a collector that is not running yet. The file is a
# dpkg conffile, so your edit survives package upgrades.
#
# To activate: remove the "#" from the last line. Nothing else needs
# changing - the helper path below is filled in by the packaging, so it
# matches wherever this package actually installed the helper.
#
# The file name carries no dot on purpose: sudo silently ignores every
# file in sudoers.d whose name contains a "." or ends in "~". A copy
# saved as "my-xymon-extensions-server.bak" is therefore not a backup,
# it is an inactive file - and one saved as "...-server.conf" would
# never be read at all.
#
# Other packagings (rpm, FreeBSD) do not install this file. Put it in
# place yourself, and validate before and after:
#
#   visudo -c -f powerline.sudoers
#   install -o root -g root -m 0440 powerline.sudoers \
#       /etc/sudoers.d/my-xymon-extensions-server
#
# WHAT THE RULE GRANTS. powerline-read.sh is the only privileged part of
# the collector. It is root-owned, validates operation, interface and
# MAC, fixes the paths of plcstat and plcrate, clears the tool
# environment and permits only topology, PHY and peer-statistics reads,
# each with a fixed 15-second timeout.
#
# It is only as safe as its own path: if the xymon user can write the
# helper, or ANY directory on the way to it, this rule is a root shell.
# The server package's postinst checks that and reports what it finds.
#
# Never grant sudo for powerline.sh itself, for a shell, or for
# unrestricted plctool / open-plc-utils commands.

#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: @BINDIR@/powerline-read.sh
